## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of basic concurrency primitives, memory ordering, and a practical IPC primitive from first principles.

## Key Concepts
- Bounded ring buffer fundamentals
- Atomic indices and memory ordering
- Data visibility vs. race-free updates
- Simple correctness testing under contention

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo C-like sketch
#define N 1024
int buf[N];
atomic_size_t head = 0, tail = 0;

int enqueue(int v){
  size_t t = atomic_load_explicit(&tail, memory_order_relaxed);
  size_t h = atomic_load_explicit(&head, memory_order_acquire);
  size_t next = (t + 1) % N;
  if (next == h) return -1; // full
  buf[t] = v;
  atomic_store_explicit(&tail, next, memory_order_release);
  return 0;
}

int dequeue(int *out){
  size_t h = atomic_load_explicit(&head, memory_order_relaxed);
  size_t t = atomic_load_explicit(&tail, memory_order_acquire);
  if (h == t) return -1; // empty
  *out = buf[h];
  atomic_store_explicit(&head, (h + 1) % N, memory_order_release);
  return 0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to multiple producers/consumers?
- How would you detect and recover from stack/memory ordering mistakes?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical IPC with shared memory and demonstrates mastery of memory ordering in a real, implementable primitive.

## Key Concepts
- Single-producer/single-consumer lock-free design
- Atomic operations and acquire/release semantics
- Memory visibility across cores and cache-line padding
- mmap-based IPC and data layout in shared memory

## Code Example
```c
// Simplified sketch (not production-ready)
typedef struct {
  uint64_t head; // read index
  uint64_t tail; // write index
  char data[4096];
} ring_t;

void prod(ring_t *r, const void *src, size_t n){
  uint64_t t = __atomic_load_n(&r->tail, __ATOMIC_RELAXED);
  // write data to slot (omitting slot indexing details)
  memcpy(r->data + (t % 1024) * 4, src, n);
  __atomic_store_n(&r->tail, t+1, __ATOMIC_RELEASE);
}

void cons(ring_t *r, void *dst, size_t n){
  uint64_t t = __atomic_load_n(&r->tail, __ATOMIC_ACQUIRE);
  uint64_t h = __atomic_load_n(&r->head, __ATOMIC_RELAXED);
  if (h < t){
    memcpy(dst, r->data + (h % 1024) * 4, n);
    __atomic_store_n(&r->head, h+1, __ATOMIC_RELEASE);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for multiple producers? discuss ring partitioning or a per-slot sequence counter.
- What changes for varying message sizes or overflow handling? discuss padding and data-copy costs.

