## Why This Is Asked

Multi-tenant I/O contention tests practical OS scheduling design, fairness guarantees, and real workload handling on a block device.

## Key Concepts

- per-tenant queues
- credit-based throttling
- burst handling
- fairness metrics

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal sketch: enqueue/dequeue with per-tenant credits (pseudo-C)
class TenantQueue {
  constructor(creditLimit) {
    this.credits = creditLimit;
    this.queue = [];
  }
  enqueue(req) { this.queue.push(req); }
  dequeue() { return this.queue.shift(); }
}
function chooseNext(tenants) {
  // pick queue with max deficit ratio: (creditUsed / max(credit,1))
  let best = null; let bestScore = -Infinity;
  for (const t of tenants) {
    const score = t.credits <= 0 ? -Infinity : 1 - t.credits / t.creditLimit;
    if (score > bestScore) { bestScore = score; best = t; }
  }
  return best && best.queue.length ? best.dequeue() : null;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt for non-uniform I/O sizes?
- How would you test for tail-latency regression under burst bursts?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical lock design for multi-socket systems, balancing scalability and correctness under contention.

## Key Concepts

- MCS locks and queue-based synchronization
- NUMA-aware data locality
- Cache-line padding and false sharing avoidance
- Timeout/backoff to prevent livelock

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo C-like sketch for acquire/release
typedef struct Node { volatile int locked; struct Node *next; } Node;
typedef struct Lock { Node *tail; } MCSLock;
void lock_acquire(MCSLock *L, Node *me) {
  me->next = NULL; me->locked = 1;
  Node *pred = atomic_swap(&L->tail, me);
  if (pred) { pred->next = me; while (me->locked) pause(); }
}
void lock_release(MCSLock *L, Node *me) {
  if (!me->next) { if (atomic_compare_and_swap(&L->tail, me, NULL)) return; while (!me->next) pause(); }
  me->next->locked = 0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend for reentrancy or per-thread timeouts?
- How does this scale when threads migrate between NUMA nodes?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests deep understanding of io_uring's concurrency model, memory ordering semantics, and real-world race conditions in high-performance I/O systems.

## Key Concepts
- io_uring submission/completion ring ordering semantics
- Cross-core memory barriers and NUMA-aware synchronization
- Per-I/O sequencing for completion tracking
- Ring buffer management under burst conditions
- Atomic operations for completion ring updates

## Code Example
```c
// Pseudo-C outline showing proper barriers
struct io_slot *s = &ring->slots[prod_head];

// Ensure data is visible before updating ring indices
atomic_store_explicit(&s->data, completion_data, memory_order_release);
atomic_store_explicit(&ring->prod_tail, new_tail, memory_order_release);

// Consumer side with acquire semantics
uint32_t tail = atomic_load_explicit(&ring->prod_tail, memory_order_acquire);
completion_data = atomic_load_explicit(&s->data, memory_order_acquire);
```

## Performance Trade-offs
Per-I/O sequence numbers add minimal overhead (8 bytes per operation) but provide strong correctness guarantees. The atomic operations introduce ~5-10ns latency per completion, which is negligible compared to NVMe access times (~100μs). However, under extreme burst conditions, contention on the completion ring indices can become a bottleneck, potentially requiring sharding or per-core completion queues.

